{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1194964-how-is-cod-played","id":1194964,"title":"How is CoD played?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-03-13T13:37:28-07:00","id":1194964,"title":"How is CoD played?","content":"I stumbled across this and I agree. <br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/showthread.php?825291-VtM-How-is-Vampire-typically-played\" target=\"_blank\">https://forum.rpg.net/showthread.php...pically-played</a><br /> <br /> Specifically the fifth poster: <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\"> <span style=\"font-family:Tahoma\"> By what the books claim to want, Vampire is intended to be these character-focused stories about struggling to avoid becoming a monster, while navigating and manuevering through a dangerous political landscape. <br /> <br /> But then the game fails to support any of that mechanically in any way that really makes it usable without the group having to do all the work themselves.<br /> <br /> And about half of the clans are combat-oriented.<br /> <br /> So typically, Vampire just kind of turns into a bunch of violence (often involving like gangs and the Sabbat and stuff), basically DnD-style murderhobos, but in a dark and broody modern city instead of in a dungeon. <br /> <br /> Vampire is kind of tbh a really big example of a total failure of game design, because of the fact that the game wants something, fails to reinforce that thing through anything but words and lore dumps, and then people play it differently and the designers are confused on what they did wrong.<br /> <br /> </span><br /> </span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">Unfortunately yes. The nWoD or now the Chronicles of Darkness just does this with a more streamlined and coherent rule-set. The latest 2nd edition books for Vampire the Requiem, etc., incorporate some narrative game elements. But they feel pasted on top of the existing Storyteller System rules. I'm talking about the new Aspiration, Beat, Conditions system. I think the additions are a step in the right direction in terms of fulfilling the promise in the prefaces of all the games.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">Here is literally the first paragraph out of character in the New World of Darkness Core book.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b>This book presents rules for playing a type of roleplaying game called Storytelling. In this type of game, the traditional elements of a story -- theme, mood, plot and character -- are more important than the rules themselves. The rules serve to help you tell stories about your characters in an interactive experience. They help prevent arguments and provide a solid basis for handling elements of chance, but they don't overshadow the story itself. The triumphs and tragedies of your characters as they try to survive and even thrive in the World of Darkness are the main focus, not dice rolls or lists of traits.</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">It then proceeds to give a wealth of crunch dealing with firing guns in close combat, etc.</span></span> </div> </div> </div>How would people here agree or disagree with this? <br /> <br /> Thanks!<br /> <br /> edg<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5581-evildmguy","name":"evildmguy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5581_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-13T13:45:43-07:00","id":1194965,"title":"","content":"Personally I find vampire to be among the more well written of the lines, not that others aren't good, and Hunter is my favorite, but I actually found vampire to be really good at nailing down what it wanted to do. Sure there are probably improvements to be made but I'm not knowledgeable enough to point them out and at the end of the day it was never really &quot;marketed&quot; towards me to begin with. I think most of the game lines that I have experience in,(so not beast, demon, Geist, mummy, Promethean and changeling as I have never looked into those and can't really say,) did what they set about to do very well, whether one likes what they set out to do or if they would have gone about it differently is another matter. Combat is always going no to take up a large portion of rule books like this because combat is complicated.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13063-lord-jub-jub","name":"Lord Jub-Jub","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13063_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"121"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-13T14:02:01-07:00","id":1194970,"title":"","content":"Checking out the link they seem to have some sort of issue with the game.<br /> <br /> Which explains why they are exaggerating things like 'a wealth of crunch dealing with firing guns in close combat, etc'<br /> <br /> People seem to find the Beat thing hit or miss. But it's intent is to help players be more willing to take drawbacks to their character. The one who compared it to Game of Thrones was right, and some examples from there would be Bran losing his legs, Jaime his hand, and various other setbacks characters have come across.<br /> <br /> Generally when I run it things tend towards more social thrillers, there's sometimes action but it's rarely the big draw of the game, compared to the connections PCs make to NPCs and each other.<br /> <br /> That said, 'how CoD is played' depends a lot on the table. You can have Scooby Doo-ish mystery investigators who never harm a soul. You can also have John Woo-like action games full of gunplay and expendable enemies (there are rules for different guns, they're just in a different book for people who like lots of different kinds of guns). Games can be run like Bram Stoker's Dracula or Blade, like Byzantium or 30 Days of Night. It's up to the group involved and whatever way you go I think you would have to work hard or make a point of being obnoxious about it to have the developer tell you you're doing it wrong.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-13T17:50:20-07:00","id":1195053,"title":"","content":"One of the things about the CofD, is that it exists (and I think most of us fans like it here) in a strange place most RPGs reject. Most RPGs are either focused on a very specific playstyle (from good old D&amp;D to something really different like Dread), or try to be as general as possible (GURPS or Fate are both designed to be whatever you want them to be if in very different approaches). The CofD, and the WoD before it, have always tried to balance a strange medium between those two places. The CofD doesn't try to be everything. There's game styles it clearly doesn't cater to. But it style aims to be a bigger tent than more focused games. The CofD can do a lot of different modes of play well, as long as you stick somewhere in the horror/urban fantasy/maybe low fantasy alternate setting areas.<br /> <br /> This does, however, make it hard to answer the question of &quot;how is CofD played?&quot; It's designed to be played in a bunch of ways. For all that some people don't like certain parts of 2e, there are elements in the game to help guide the group in where to go. Aspirations are one of those. With good communication between the ST and players regarding Aspirations, you can really see how a given game is at least going to start out in play.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-13T19:30:52-07:00","id":1195081,"title":"","content":"I really am curious about this and I think these answers, while appreciated, missed the mark. <br /> <br /> How do the RAW of oWoD, nWoD, CoD, support the bolded part of my post? Where in the rules are the rules for how to achieve the theme of a vampire fighting against his humanity? <br /> <br /> Let me look at VtR for my examples. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Beast: The inchoate urges that drive vampires away from the Man.<br /> Man, the: The humanity a vampire maintains (or tries to); the spark of mortality that distinguishes him from the Beast. VtR p 85 </div> </div> </div><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Humanity: How the Kindred stave off the wiles of the Beast Within, and the toll its actions take upon their sense of morality. <br /> Derangements: When a Kindred\u2019s mental or moral state erodes, she sometimes exhibits dangerous psychological quirks. VtR p157 </div> </div> </div>Okay, let's start with this. An earlier description tells how mechanically, Humanity replaces the CoD Morality. However, a Vampire's Morality starts at seven. SEVEN! On a ten point scale, that seems high for a vampire. Character creation mentions it on p82 and that they can start lower for xp, down to a five. Still at half, for a creature that has a Beast that only wants what it wants, in the description of the Beast. <br /> <br /> Humanity, even though it is talked about a lot, isn't fully defined until p181. In it, we find that a seven rating means that a Petty Theft will require a roll to lose humanity. So, we have, in the rules, a character who is defined as a monster with a Beast but is pretty high on the humanity scale. <br /> <br /> I would posit that the system is already not doing well in portraying Vampires as fighting against a darker nature. If they started at a Humanity of 4 and could buy it up, that would indicate that for me. At a 4, they can commit manslaughter, you know that thing you might have to do to get blood. <br /> <br /> If they do have to make a Humanity roll and it's a degeneration, they might suffer a derangement. Those start on p186 with some examples on p 188. These are good examples. If a vampire does gain one, these are good. They have a description but then a game effect that can and should be enforced. However. I have played a long time and giving a character a penalty like this, imx, doesn't work. The player will merely have their character avoid situations that would call for a roll. Further, a lot of it could be situational. Delusional Obsession, for example, gives an example of a gardening obsession. (How many have played Vampires with that?) And then an obsession with anything to do with the Moon. They are really cool and evocative but it's part role playing and it sets up GM v player, imo, rather than helping the story. I'm not arguing that the player could bring this up themselves. <b><i>I'm saying that the RAW gives them no incentive to do so! </i></b><br /> <br /> While the derangements are good, there are no good examples of Humanity loss. Where does feeding fit? If they don't kill, or intend to kill, do they get to keep their starting 7? Doesn't that seem high? I have read some people like to get into the social and political side of it. Where is a white collar crime? Robbing a bank on the scale of hacking, not a gun robbery? Insurance fraud? Extortion? Manipulation? What about slavery or human trafficking? Some of those are implied to be on there but by not being on there, it leaves those in a grey area, which again goes back to GM v player. <br /> <br /> Willpower is powerful. It seems to let you overcome bad things. If the chronicle is an action packed thing with something happening every day, I could see Willpower getting low and then not having it to be able to resist something that would stop humanity. Is this common? I ask because the game sets up a long running campaign with immortal characters. Basically, this is the five minute adventure day that most systems have. Does the GM need to keep the pressure up constantly? Or can the players have their characters rest until any Willpower is full and no chance to lose Humanity? Or, anything? Can they rest until fine, whether that's days or weeks, and then get back to their plans? <br /> <br /> While I grant that combat gets a lot of attention because it can take a while, I found combat in Mage to be some of the fastest and best dramatic system out there. Further, for a game of socializing, where are the social combat rules? Where are the status rules? The setup of a city literally fills all of the positions in the &quot;government&quot; of the Kindred, forcing them to accept they are the lowest or to move to someplace else. Conflict is purely physical to climb the ladder without social rules. Again, a group could role play this! I'm not arguing that.<i><b> I'm asking where in the RAW this is shown? </b></i><br /> <br /> Finally, I don't see any system about the Beast. Just that losing control causes the Beast to emerge, perhaps in a Frenzy. There is not even a listing for Beast in the index! For something that seems to be central to the game, it's got a definition and then is mentioned with other things but is left vague itself. <br /> <br /> So, that's my point about playing a Vampire. The RAW doesn't seem to support a horror theme. In contrast, FATE allows you to put your character into a bad situation to gain a Fate point, which can be used later. Mutants and Masterminds doesn't give the characters a Hero Point until they have felt the disadvantage! No freebies there. <br /> <br /> I do see a lot of talk about humanity but the system of losing it, or the Beast, just isn't well defined. Further, ratings are such that each point matters and so players are going to resist as well as they can, using ideas above and I'm sure other things, to not risk &quot;losing&quot; their humanity. And that point, it doesn't seem to be a horror game to me. <br /> <br /> While I have my own problems with Exalted's Limit Break, and don't know 3E very well, Exalted's Limit Break idea would seem to be a better starting point. It's still left vague but a meter that causes the character to truly make tough choices would be better than what I have seen in my time with Vampire. Imagine a system where anytime they are under pressure the meter fills. Slowly they watch that meter rise, knowing they will have to make a tough call. Further, couple it with combat (or social combat), so it's not something that will ever happen off screen or in downtime. It will happen at the worst possible time. Is that great drama? And where in the system is that? <br /> <br /> I hope this better explains my thought process on why I agreed with the post I had. I do appreciate the feedback and agree that a game can be whatever the group makes of it. I want to know what the RAW does to create the mood, theme, plot, and character it set out to do. <br /> <br /> Thanks!<br /> <br /> edg","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5581-evildmguy","name":"evildmguy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5581_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-13T21:22:11-07:00","id":1195112,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>evildmguy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1194964-how-is-cod-played?p=1195081#post1195081\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">snip</div> </div> </div> </div>I'll leave it to other posters to go into this in detail, but you seem to still be going off the first edition, so I don't know that these criticisms are entirely fair? There <i>is </i>a Social maneuvering (rather than combat) system, for example. Or the fact that vampires no longer gain Willpower as easily. In fact, a lot of the changes in Second Edition address many of your points.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><b>Social justice vampire/freelancer | He/Him</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Actual Play:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/852858-vampire-the-requiem-%E2%80%93-bloodlines-ap\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Requiem \u2013 Bloodlines</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Masquiem:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/5268-13-licks-the-curses-of-caine-in-blood-smoke\" target=\"_blank\">Curses of Caine in Requiem 2nd</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Storytellers Vault: </b></span><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=None%20More%20Dark%20Publishing#affiliate_id=321925\" target=\"_blank\">Author Page</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","name":"Yossarian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-13T22:47:59-07:00","id":1195139,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>evildmguy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1194964-how-is-cod-played#post1194964\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I stumbled across this and I agree.<br /> <br /> &quot;This book presents rules for playing a type of roleplaying game called Storytelling. In this type of game, the traditional elements of a story -- theme, mood, plot and character -- are more important than the rules themselves. The rules serve to help you tell stories about your characters in an interactive experience. They help prevent arguments and provide a solid basis for handling elements of chance, but they don't overshadow the story itself. The triumphs and tragedies of your characters as they try to survive and even thrive in the World of Darkness are the main focus, not dice rolls or lists of traits.&quot;<br /> <br /> How would people here agree or disagree with this?</div> </div> </div> </div>It's pretty agreeable, except the first sentence.<br /> <br /> Vampire the Masquerade used the Storyteller System. Vampire the Requiem uses the Storytelling System. They're systems.<br /> <br /> From there Masquerade and Requiem do have some linked ideas and terminology, but are pretty much entirely different games. Masquerade deals with new players being caught up in a setting depicting an age-old battle between ancient enemies and their machinations while also juggling their own hunger to be a monster and the political atmosphere around them. Vampire the Requiem gets rid of most of that setting, placing you more in a world where your character/coterie is the centerpiece, dials down the Curse, and balances out the socio-political atmosphere, being a vampire has its own highs and lows.<br /> <br /> As for the rest, yeah it's a pretty impressive write-up but aside from being based on an older edition also seems to be working under a few misconceptions, like manslaughter being needed to feed, that the human to corpse to undead to functioning member of the All Night's Society wouldn't affect one's Humanity, that the RAW offers no incentive to engage in one's character despite it being a source of much of your experience (and thus mechanical growth) even in first edition and so on.<br /> <br /> Check out second edition, but you seem to already have your mind well set. You can play vampires in FATE if you want, no one here is going to mind.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 03-14-2018, 01:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-14T02:21:54-07:00","id":1195190,"title":"","content":"Not really in a mood to go through this entire thing because as noted most of the valid stuff is addressed in 2e anyway.<br /> <br /> I will also make the point in general that VtR was the <b>second</b> nWoD book ever, developed at the same time as the nWoD core book. There's a lot of early installment weirdness in it, and there's a lot of supplements that address things lacking in the core book (esp.: Damnation City, the Chronicler's Guide, and Requiem for Rome for VtR, and Asylum and Mirrors for general rules relevant to the points being made). If &quot;there's a supplement that covers it,&quot; isn't good enough beyond the whole 2e thing, not sure what else to say.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>evildmguy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1194964-how-is-cod-played?p=1195081#post1195081\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">However, a Vampire's Morality starts at seven.</div> </div> </div> </div>This whole thing confuses me.<br /> <br /> So what? Seven is a starting point, largely because it's where humans on Morality start, because VtR starting level vampires are by default fairly new to the whole vampire thing. You're not making someone that's been stalking the nights for a few centuries (hence the optional starting XP chart for making more experienced characters), you're making a character that's somewhere between a pretty competent person that was Embraced very recently, or a vampire that was more of an average person but has had some time to adjust and learn about vampire society basics (a lot of this will be in things like Merit selection).<br /> <br /> The starting Humanity level on the higher side leaves their room for it to fall before the character's are either stable with their condition or falling hard. If you started Humanity at 4, the actions that even put it at risk are atrocious enough that avoiding them isn't that hard. You'd eliminate struggling with the Beast completely. The Humanity 4-7 range is largely where you're going to see the most struggling because it's were there's the most room for moral compromises that people are willing to risk. If you changed VtR 1e to start at Humanity 4.. you might as well just ignore Humanity change completely and set anything that treats Humanity are a variable to 4; regaining Humanity is difficulty and expensive, while losing it from there requires just giving in to being a monster.<br /> <br /> Humanity is pretty much going to down to at least 4 for most players over time unless they're actively throwing XP (and a lot of it) to keep it high. Eventually they're going to fail rolls.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They are really cool and evocative but it's part role playing and it sets up GM v player, imo, </div> </div> </div>And this whole bit is too subjective. &quot;GM v player&quot; is an attitude of the group. All RPGs set up the GM/etc. in the role of providing the challenge of the game for the players. Not matter how much a gaming book encourages an antagonistic relationship, or a cooperative one, having a GM/etc. cannot be mechanically forced into a cooperative style. I've seen people make D&amp;D feel super-collaborative because the DM loves to see the PC's shine and sets up really fun scenarios with player input to make that happen. I've seen Fate get super antagonistic over the GM's approach to compels.<br /> <br /> It's a position that's only as problematically antagonistic as the group makes it. Even with mechanical incentives (which there are, even if not as blatant as 2e makes them) some players avoid this stuff. Even without them, some players love it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Where does feeding fit? </div> </div> </div>Where ever is appropriate based on the method of feeding. Feeding a small amount from a willing donor is obviously not the same level of Humanity sin as grabbing a random person of the street and feeding to the point of killing them. Though the feeding rules do make it difficult to never screw up and overfeed (and other problems as Blood Potency rises).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Willpower is powerful. </div> </div> </div>It's a limited source of +3 dice to rolls, or +2 to Defense/Resistance. It's not that great. Also there's things you can't spend it on; Humanity checks being a fairly important one to note here (most of your point seems to have missed that). It's also spent on a bunch of things besides general roll boosting. Even if you're playing a game with a relaxed enough time frame to regain Willpower to full easily... it's still limited enough that players are going to weight spending it rather than spamming it, but not feel too bad if they spent it all and can't push one roll a bit higher.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Finally, I don't see any system about the Beast. </div> </div> </div>The Humanity. hunger, and Frenzy systems are about the Beast. The Beast is defined by how it's resisted.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Imagine a system where anytime they are under pressure the meter fills. </div> </div> </div>And, IME, these things suck for horror games. It works for Exalted (actually it really didn't work that well for Exalted until 3e) because Exalted isn't a horror game, and that build up is to highlight that the Exalted are flawed beings and evoke inspirational material where heroes frequently flipped out and did grandly irrational things to match their grand heroics. In horror games, it just turns into, &quot;count down until the game screws me no matter what I did.&quot; It's kinda fun in Dread, but Dread is a horror-themed party game with some RPG-like elements. You're not going to play a long running campaign of Dread and have it keep the tension of the primary mechanic fun.<br /> <br /> Long term horror RPG games need to focus on character choices and player agency to maintain a sense of drama. Those generate player buy-in, which generates engagement with the story, which generates horror themed drama. Otherwise horror quickly devolves into resource management<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-14T05:54:00-07:00","id":1195245,"title":"","content":"These were awesome responses! Thanks! <br /> <br /> Correct, I don't have 2E VtR. I barely have much of God Machine Chronicle, if that's the same. <br /> <br /> I'm asking because I'm a &quot;victim&quot; of DND/PF and those have shaped my gaming lens. It's only now that I am starting to truly grok other systems, such as Shadowrun, and I wanted to do the same with CoD. We are currently playing a superhero game in Mutants and Masterminds, although Aberrant was a close contender for the system to use, and M&amp;M comes with a surprisingly strict system that you don't get anything for free. You don't get a Hero Point until you have already put yourself in danger. Compared to get free points for giving a character a phobia of small locked rooms higher than the 37th floor with snakes AND spiders and then how often does that come up? And that's not a dig at the storytelling system as many systems do that. <br /> <br /> My players are all my friends and I wouldn't have it any other way. I'm the one who's heavy into RPGs, not them. When I have tried non Hunter type games in WoD/CoD, I feel like I failed because I didn't evoke the horror mood or how they are fighting the Beast. My players never brought up the horror they think their characters are going through in becoming a monster. If the game has a system in place in its RAW where they can't get a bonus until they push the character, only then they will use it. That's what I was wondering if I had missed such a system in the game. What all of your responses tell me is exactly what I wanted, which is to know how to present the game to my players to see if they are interested in it. Thanks! <br /> <br /> I am going to touch on a few things but I wanted to share why I was looking for a horror mechanic in the RAW. <br /> <br /> I wasn't saying that manslaughter was needed for a vampire to feed. What I was saying is that I don't remember if the game ever discusses long term effects of thinking of humans as food. Can that wear on vampires? I mean, I assume the designers weren't making a case for real world vegetarianism with this, in saying that by eating meat, we lose our humanity. But can the same be said of vampires? Is this an analogy of racism? elitism? Or is it dialed back to if there is no killing, it's all fine? <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Vampire the Masquerade used the Storyteller System. Vampire the Requiem uses the Storytelling System. They're systems.<br /> <br /> From there Masquerade and Requiem do have some linked ideas and terminology, but are pretty much entirely different games. Masquerade deals with new players being caught up in a setting depicting an age-old battle between ancient enemies and their machinations while also juggling their own hunger to be a monster and the political atmosphere around them. Vampire the Requiem gets rid of most of that setting, placing you more in a world where your character/coterie is the centerpiece, dials down the Curse, and balances out the socio-political atmosphere, being a vampire has its own highs and lows. </div> </div> </div>Wow. Very subtle and I never noticed that difference from Storyteller to Storytelling. Is that the difference between being at the bottom and having to survive vs potentially being able to move up the ladder? But a good point here. Thanks! <br /> <br /> I'm sure we will consider the Storytelling system again and I wanted to be ready to better explain it to my players. Thanks! <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So what? Seven is a starting point, largely because it's where humans on Morality start, because VtR starting level vampires are by default fairly new to the whole vampire thing. You're not making someone that's been stalking the nights for a few centuries (hence the optional starting XP chart for making more experienced characters), you're making a character that's somewhere between a pretty competent person that was Embraced very recently, or a vampire that was more of an average person but has had some time to adjust and learn about vampire society basics (a lot of this will be in things like Merit selection). </div> </div> </div>That's fair and a good clarification. I mentioned it because I'm surprised that a mortal who was considered for the Embrace would be that human? Does that make sense? I'm being overly general but it seems that a good Ventrue, for example, would be an up and coming manager, who has maybe done shady things to get where they are. I guess I'm coming at this from the perspective that it's is emphasized a lot (I'm thinking of the game Bloodlines) that to make a vampire is a &quot;gift&quot; from the Prince and not something that happens often. Therefore, it seemed to me that if most vampires end up in the 4-7 range, probably around 5(?), that they would be careful in who they pick and would want to make sure they already know their reaction. Hence, me thinking their soon to be childe would have already lost a bit of Humanity. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Willpower </div> </div> </div>The reason I was thinking Willpower is powerful is because I thought it could be used to either write a scene or auto succeed? If I'm mixing my editions, then oops! <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Humanity. hunger, and Frenzy systems are about the Beast. The Beast is defined by how it's resisted. </div> </div> </div>Okay. Does that mean the designers wanted to leave it up to individuals how their characters' Beast manifests? What it is? How often they see it? Is the Beast an external entity from the character? Or just the darkest parts of the character made manifest at weak moments? In any case, a good clarification! <br /> <br /> This goes back to me being into RPGs more than my players. I would jump into this. I would have a background written up and think about each time I get close to the Beast. What's my Vice? Anger? Now that he has power, does he become the bully? I do all of that but for the most part, my players want to react, not act. That's fine! I understand it falls onto me to understand the system and explain it to them, which is the reason for my questions here. <br /> <br /> And you guys did not disappoint! Awesome! <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And, IME, these things suck for horror games. It works for Exalted (actually it really didn't work that well for Exalted until 3e) because Exalted isn't a horror game, and that build up is to highlight that the Exalted are flawed beings and evoke inspirational material where heroes frequently flipped out and did grandly irrational things to match their grand heroics. In horror games, it just turns into, &quot;count down until the game screws me no matter what I did.&quot; It's kinda fun in Dread, but Dread is a horror-themed party game with some RPG-like elements. You're not going to play a long running campaign of Dread and have it keep the tension of the primary mechanic fun.<br /> <br /> Long term horror RPG games need to focus on character choices and player agency to maintain a sense of drama. Those generate player buy-in, which generates engagement with the story, which generates horror themed drama. Otherwise horror quickly devolves into resource management </div> </div> </div>And these are also really good points! Thanks! (And I have the main 3E book of Exalted and need to look into how they did it there.) <br /> <br /> I am curious as to what you mean by resource management? Or does that go back to my point of making sure that are &quot;fresh&quot; before confronting something? <br /> <br /> Maybe what I was looking for was something like Insanity rules that are in Call of Cthulhu type games to show a slow loss of sanity? Or maybe I wasn't understanding how much is up to the players to bring the horror to the table? <br /> <br /> I brought up Exalted precisely for what you said above. In Exalted, that Limit Break meter was a source of dread for the players. They didn't like it, which means that either I shouldn't have picked Exalted to run or ignored it for what they did enjoy. Anyway. As that Limit Break filled and as the player saw their character getting closer and closer to a bad thing happening, I really felt that put them in the mood of Exalted. Of being godlike and wanting to help but having this curse, this darkness, that lingers. I thought that was a great way for the RAW to show what was meant for Exalted. I was looking for that in VtR and maybe it's not there. Or maybe it is in second edition but I don't have that. <br /> <br /> This has really helped me a lot! Thanks a million! Two cookies for all! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5581-evildmguy","name":"evildmguy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5581_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-14T07:24:51-07:00","id":1195287,"title":"","content":"There's little concern for morality in games like D&amp;D save, what? 15% of the classes involved (Paladins, Druids, Clerics) at most? Don't blame your players if they're used to shrug and go thru the motions when their Hounds tell them to kill the otherwise innocent masquerade breach witness. Or when they fail a frenzy roll and drain a kid completely. D&amp;D and PF makes you think about scenarios tactically, not dramatically. &quot;How do I hide this body?&quot; is the first thing they'll think, not &quot;FUCK WHY DID I DO THIS?!&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-14T08:31:03-07:00","id":1195304,"title":"","content":"My personal problem with focusing on humanity and morality is probably similar to your players problem in focusing on the struggle with the beast.<br /> <br /> I for one think that a game about struggling against an inner beast and not loosing ones humanity is perfectly well served by simply playing humans. Put a human in the right situation and the above is exactly what would happen. So I do not really see where vampires come in to serve those themes any better then ordinary humans already do.<br /> <br /> On the other hand if I play a game about vampires, I am kind of more interested in actually playing an inhuman vampire, not a vampire who tries to be as human as possible.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23979-traldar","name":"Traldar","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-14T08:45:17-07:00","id":1195312,"title":"","content":"I'd say the additions made in 2e are an admission that the original system did not live up to its goals. They are mostly cribbing from narrative games. Aspirations and Conditions are different sorts of Fate Aspects, and Beats incentivize their use, with experience rather than with equivalent of Willpower that Fate Points give you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5092-quazar87","name":"Quazar87","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5092_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"18"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-14T08:46:53-07:00","id":1195314,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Traldar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1194964-how-is-cod-played?p=1195304#post1195304\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I for one think that a game about struggling against an inner beast and not loosing ones humanity is perfectly well served by simply playing humans. Put a human in the right situation and the above is exactly what would happen. So I do not really see where vampires come in to serve those themes any better then ordinary humans already do.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're thinking about this backwards. Requiem isn't about vampires to serve themes; the themes serve to make the vampire fiction more interesting. It gives a degree of balance to stop the thing just tipping into complete power-fantasy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-14T09:26:45-07:00","id":1195327,"title":"","content":"<br /> Oh I'm perfectly fine with humanity being there as counterbalance. As something to as something to struggle with to not be completely consumed.<br /> <br /> I just wanted to point that, when players hear let's play vampires, they might be more interested in exploring the inhumane aspects of that condition, instead of trying to stay as human and moral as possible.<br /> <br /> If that is the case it is hard to convey a sense of personal horror trough a descent of humanity and the struggle with the beast, since being the beast is part of what makes the experience interesting then.<br /> This seems to be important since the Op feels that he failed to convey a sense of personal horror through the system.<br /> <br /> As an aside, I feel like the theme of personal horror is best served in VtR by playing an elder. Your are so far removed from humanity and ordinary life that loosing yourself to the beast becomes a danger. The lure of Torpor hangs over you like a damocles sword. You have power, but you must guard it well or you might lose it. Or you have already gone through stretches of torpor once, multiple times? Your memory is... scattered. You remember having incredible power at your fingertips, but now your blood is weak. Some young imposter could tear you apart, and he might just do so if he ever found out what you really are. And the world has changed, so much and it seems to change so much with every night that passes. Have you really been asleep for just one day?<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23979-traldar","name":"Traldar","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-14T10:02:43-07:00","id":1195347,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Traldar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1194964-how-is-cod-played?p=1195304#post1195304\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I for one think that a game about struggling against an inner beast and not loosing ones humanity is perfectly well served by simply playing humans. Put a human in the right situation and the above is exactly what would happen. So I do not really see where vampires come in to serve those themes any better then ordinary humans already do.</div> </div> </div> </div>What vampirism adds is the guarantee that the player characters are always in this &quot;right situation&quot; you speak of.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-14T10:20:08-07:00","id":1195355,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>evildmguy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1194964-how-is-cod-played?p=1195245#post1195245\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's fair and a good clarification. I mentioned it because I'm surprised that a mortal who was considered for the Embrace would be that human? Does that make sense? I'm being overly general but it seems that a good Ventrue, for example, would be an up and coming manager, who has maybe done shady things to get where they are. I guess I'm coming at this from the perspective that it's is emphasized a lot (I'm thinking of the game Bloodlines) that to make a vampire is a &quot;gift&quot; from the Prince and not something that happens often. Therefore, it seemed to me that if most vampires end up in the 4-7 range, probably around 5(?), that they would be careful in who they pick and would want to make sure they already know their reaction. Hence, me thinking their soon to be childe would have already lost a bit of Humanity.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well for one thing it doesn't matter how 'shady' a human is, they're still human. And taking bribes or firing people for unjust reasons are things that will effect you differently than, again, dying, returning as a corpse with none of the usual 'human' things like breathing, itches, the need to blink, eat food and so forth. If you're thinking of the game bloodlines, then you might want to go into the Vampire the Masquerade forum, because like I said it's a different game with different setting conceits, and you seem to be leaning big on one. In Requiem new vampires are not always thoroughly vetted and know what they're getting into. The clan doesn't have that much control over who gets embraced. Masquerade had something like a 20 year internship for joining the Ventrue. Requiem you could become one by accident.<br /> <br /> There's also the fact that the game is not meant to start 5 seconds after your Embrace. By the time you start the game, you have learned disciplines (magic powers based on bending your beast to your will), joined a covenant, learned how to feed and developed your own method for doing so. These things take a toll too.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Okay. Does that mean the designers wanted to leave it up to individuals how their characters' Beast manifests? What it is? How often they see it? Is the Beast an external entity from the character? Or just the darkest parts of the character made manifest at weak moments? In any case, a good clarification! </div> </div> </div>Beasts are different depending on the person, it's not an external entity, I'm concerned you're now thinking of Beast the Primordial now, another game entirely. In vampire, a beast is their natural vampire instinct. It wants you to throw away chains of humanity and run wild, though they have different ideas of what run wild mean, it usually means the death of those who would resist you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-14T10:31:43-07:00","id":1195362,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1194964-how-is-cod-played?p=1195287#post1195287\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's little concern for morality in games like D&amp;D save, what? 15% of the classes involved (Paladins, Druids, Clerics) at most? Don't blame your players if they're used to shrug and go thru the motions when their Hounds tell them to kill the otherwise innocent masquerade breach witness. Or when they fail a frenzy roll and drain a kid completely. D&amp;D and PF makes you think about scenarios tactically, not dramatically. &quot;How do I hide this body?&quot; is the first thing they'll think, not &quot;FUCK WHY DID I DO THIS?!&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Oh, I agree! I like the term murderhobos for PCs in DND/PF because they do answer with sword most of the time. Further, PF has stated that it is a tactical RPG. <br /> <br /> What I was wanting to know was what I was missing in the game that pushed players to ask the second question instead of the fall back on the first. It sounds like the answer is nothing, which means that, like the light bulb wanting to change, the players have to want to do this. My players aren't all as bad as I have probably made them out to be. Once in a while, they will want to do that kind of introspecting in a character. Most of the time, though, they have had a rough week and want to kill sh@t! Nothing wrong with that. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'd say the additions made in 2e are an admission that the original system did not live up to its goals. They are mostly cribbing from narrative games. Aspirations and Conditions are different sorts of Fate Aspects, and Beats incentivize their use, with experience rather than with equivalent of Willpower that Fate Points give you. </div> </div> </div>Sadly, in the Mage game I ran last year, I didn't effectively use these but I agree that looking back, this does seem to serve their purpose. Again, Fate proved too much work for all of us to use effectively. My group and I want medium rules, where we can go more or less as the feeling hits us. It seems as if these might be that but obviously I need to work more to understand them. <br /> <br /> From the RPGNet thread:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">Each player creates a certain kind of vampire that appeals to them (helping them find a concept that appeals to them, and the game widgets that go along with that concept, is the lion's share of the elevator pitch for running a game, I find). The players are encouraged to consider their survival strategy--now that you've been a vampire for a little while and you've got a grasp of what that means, how do you make it from night to night? That varies a lot from character to character, based on what Backgrounds they purchased, how they have decided those are going to work together, and what kind of character concept they're going for. All of this joins together to give each character a fairly tight set of overall long-term goals. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">Example: Player 1 is very into the horror aspect of the game. He wants to be a classic Gothic vampire, monstrous and deformed, stalking the shadows and preying on the helpless. He chooses a Nosferatu vampire. He buys two dots of the Domain Background, and defines that as territory in the city sewers. He buys two dots in Herd and two dots in Contacts, and decides that this represents a network of homeless people that he has a good deal of influence over. He blows four dots in Retainers and defines those as ghouled animals who do his bidding and act as his eyes and ears. So this Nosferatu has a defined territory, a relatively safe base of operations from which he can strike out. And he has a number of servants to act as his sources of information. If something is happening in the part of town above his sewer territory, he is probably the one vampire in town most likely to know about it. A very typical Nosferatu, with a fairly sound survival strategy. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">Player 2 wants to wield wealth and power--none of this skulking about in the sewers for her. She chooses Ventrue for her Clan, and buys two dots of Influence, and four dots each of Resources and Contacts. She decides that she is a scion of an old-money family that has been a factor in local politics for generations, ans she inherited many of her father's interests when he died (which is probably why she was chosen for the Embrace). She lives in a large and very old mansion, and she's one of the local kingmakers--you don't get to be Mayor unless you have her blessing. She's often throwing charity events in her lavish home (though she's started doing this through trusted mortal proxies lately), spreading around lots of money and giving out lots of minor favors to maintain her influence over the local power structure. She owns the local newspaper outright through a shell corporation, and although she is a silent partner and the paper itself is largely a dying legacy medium, she can make an inconvenient story just disappear every once in awhile when she really needs to. She has a minor but comfortable place in the local Camarilla hierarchy--basically, she's a pretty average Ventrue Neonate. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">(If you're wondering how all of that gets established, it's Session Zero give-and-take between the GM and the players, often in the form of the GM looking at the dots on the character sheets and asking what the player has in mind for them. &quot;You have two dots in Domain, what does that represent?&quot; &quot;I thought it could be, like, a distinct territory in the city sewers.&quot; &quot;How do you maintain control over it?&quot; &quot;I'm a scary Nosferatu; also, my Contacts and Retainers.&quot; &quot;So your guys and your beasts don't do much for you except maintain your territory, then?&quot; &quot;And gather information for me.&quot; &quot;Okay, that works for me. Now then--what does this Influence represent?&quot; &quot;She's from an old money family who has been a factor in local politics for, like, ever.&quot; &quot;Okay, that's fine. How does she fit into the local Vampire hierarchy?&quot; &quot;I thought maybe she could own a newspaper?&quot; &quot;That's not as big a deal as it used to be, you know.&quot; &quot;Yeah, but in an old-fashioned small city like this, I figured it might still be worth something.&quot; &quot;It's kind of an important thing to let a Neonate have control over--the other Ventrue are only going to let you keep it if you are very open-minded about cooperating with them.&quot; &quot;Yeah, I figured. I really see her as the type who sucks up to her superiors, and bullies her inferiors. I figure she ought to be kissing up to the local Ventrue Elders every chance she gets.&quot; &quot;Okay, that sounds good, I can work with that.&quot;)</span></span> </div> </div> </div>I'm bringing that over in case people don't have time to read it, they can at least glance at it. <br /> <br /> This seems to be a really good example of a game with player agency, of being able to come up with and play the character they were wanting. Pretending these players are real, this is really great. On the one hand, I have players thinking about their characters, what they know, and what they want. On the other hand, I don't have them coming together as a group. That puts more work on me to constantly have connections forced onto the characters. <br /> <br /> I think that's why I like how the Dresden Files RPG does character creation because baked into it are connections to the other characters as well as defining known locations for those characters. <br /> <br /> Thanks again for the replies! I really appreciate it!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5581-evildmguy","name":"evildmguy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5581_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-14T10:38:13-07:00","id":1195366,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morangias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1194964-how-is-cod-played?p=1195347#post1195347\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What vampirism adds is the guarantee that the player characters are always in this &quot;right situation&quot; you speak of.</div> </div> </div> </div>In theory yes. But then vampirism should be a wholly negative condition.<br /> As is, the superpowers vampirism comes with lean to heavy towards: vampirism is awesome!<br /> Which overshadows the struggle for humanity, as for many players the trade of is well worth the price. Add to that that most covenants at least partially glorify vampirism. Aaaand not so much.<br /> <br /> Also it is fairly easy to create a character how has no trouble to feed enough with no rick of ever having to kill someone or otherwise have to struggle to stay human. Only when plot hits, this &quot;right situation&quot;arises. Now if feeding would necessitate killing, as it does in most vampire myths, things would be different.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23979,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23979-traldar","user":"Traldar","date":". 03-14-2018, 01:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23979-traldar","name":"Traldar","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-14T12:26:00-07:00","id":1195418,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Traldar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1194964-how-is-cod-played?p=1195366#post1195366\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In theory yes. But then vampirism should be a wholly negative condition.</div> </div> </div> </div>Why would someone play that? And how could you look at vampirism myths and say things like immortality and enchantment are wholly negative?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As is, the superpowers vampirism comes with lean to heavy towards: vampirism is awesome! </div> </div> </div>That's a very shallow view of it. Vampirism has its ups and downs.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Which overshadows the struggle for humanity, as for many players the trade of is well worth the price. Add to that that most covenants at least partially glorify vampirism. Aaaand not so much. </div> </div> </div>We've had polls, very few people who actually know about the game would choose to be a vampire. The downsides outweigh the benefits and most people balk at the idea of drinking others blood to the extent vampires do. They need at least 1 Vitae a night to wake up, so you're drinking an average human once a week. Since feeding causes, at least, 1 Lethal damage a lot of players see it as a negative thing.<br /> <br /> And the covenants are support groups. They represent ways people have dealt with the chaos of life. Religion, spirituality, hierarchy, community, science. They help you deal with how to be what you are, because there's no coming back. And it's realistic. Because once you get people in a strange situation they're going to find ways to adapt to it. Even the 'sitting down and giving up' people have an option in going into torpor or becoming draugr.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also it is fairly easy to create a character how has no trouble to feed enough with no rick of ever having to kill someone or otherwise have to struggle to stay human. Only when plot hits, this &quot;right situation&quot;arises. Now if feeding would necessitate killing, as it does in most vampire myths, things would be different. </div> </div> </div>These arguments are so weird. Yes, 'the right situation' might arise. That's what plots are. That's why there's not an abundance of stories, movies, tv shows, that focus on a completely normal 9 to 5 where nothing happens except the routine. It's why even reality tv shows have scripts and go out of their way to pick dramatic people for their casts, to make things happen. And so things happen, this isn't some 'trap' Requiem pulls on you, it's expected by sitting at a table and playing a game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 03-14-2018, 03:28 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-14T12:37:02-07:00","id":1195426,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>evildmguy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1194964-how-is-cod-played?p=1195362#post1195362\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">From the RPGNet thread:</div> </div> </div> </div>Just want to point out, everything in this quote is VERY Masquerade and not all that Requiem. Those characters also don't feel much like starting characters.<br /> <br /> And it seems like Session 0 was 1-on-1 with the Storyteller and each Player. I suggest doing it as a group so that you are not later forcing the players to be together.<br /> <br /> Also, check out the Climbing the Ladder section of Requiem 2e. It is an optional character creation step for establishing your characters' interconnected connections to the local Kindred community.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>(he/him/his)<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.kickstarter.com/projects/200664283/demon-the-descent-prestige-edition\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://img513.imageshack.us/img513/4478/8vno.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> Backer #2010","edited":{"userid":1329,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1329-elvesofzion","user":"ElvesofZion","date":". 03-14-2018, 04:02 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1329-elvesofzion","name":"ElvesofZion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1329_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-14T13:21:07-07:00","id":1195444,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>evildmguy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1194964-how-is-cod-played?p=1195245#post1195245\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Correct, I don't have 2E VtR. I barely have much of God Machine Chronicle, if that's the same.</div> </div> </div> </div>GMC is a bridge book. It's written for 1e, but includes an appendix that has the essential 2e rules changes in it (you can get the appendix for free on DTRPG btw). The book titled Chronicles of Darkness is the full 2e core book, with Vampire the Requiem 2nd Edition because the full VtR 2e with changes to vampire specific stuff.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I wasn't saying that manslaughter was needed for a vampire to feed. What I was saying is that I don't remember if the game ever discusses long term effects of thinking of humans as food. </div> </div> </div>Human's aren't food. Blood is food. Humans are just the best source of food for most vampires. Losing Humanity is how the game addressing the long term effects of being a blood parasite as your character either successfully struggles to not think of humans as food and themselves as a monster, or slides into monstrosity as they become detached from humanity and start seeing the vessel and the food as the same thing. Again, you seem to be presuming a game about playing vampires that have been vampires for long enough that all these issues have been dealt with in one form or another, while the game is primarily about vampires that are still coming to terms with all this stuff.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's fair and a good clarification. I mentioned it because I'm surprised that a mortal who was considered for the Embrace would be that human? </div> </div> </div>As already noted, this is tautological. Mortals are human, regardless of how humane they acted before Embrace. That said, the character creation rules are for creating starting level vampires, and as such set arbitrary values for things. If you want to run a game where people start as humans, and are groomed by vampires, including Morality loss, and once Embraced their Morality sets their Humanity... that's fine. That's just different from what the basic character creation rules are for.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm being overly general but it seems that a good Ventrue, for example, would be an up and coming manager, who has maybe done shady things to get where they are. </div> </div> </div>A &quot;good&quot; Venture candidate is someone that understand power and how to exercise it. Morality has little to do with it save that immoral and amoral paths to power are easier to come by in the world. Covenants also matter to candidate selection mentality. A Circle of Crone Ventrue is going to look for different things than an Invictus Venture. The Crones aren't really going to care how well you can manipulate the wheels of mortal business bureaucracy, and the Invictus are going to want to see potential and ambition, not someone that's happy with a little slice of the pie.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Hence, me thinking their soon to be childe would have already lost a bit of Humanity. </div> </div> </div>Besides the VtMisms here, how can they lose something they don't have yet?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The reason I was thinking Willpower is powerful is because I thought it could be used to either write a scene or auto succeed? If I'm mixing my editions, then oops! </div> </div> </div>You're mixing games (VtM does willpower for auto-successes) not editions. Neither VtM or VtR allows you to alter the details of a scene with Willpower.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Okay. Does that mean the designers wanted to leave it up to individuals how their characters' Beast manifests? What it is? How often they see it? Is the Beast an external entity from the character? Or just the darkest parts of the character made manifest at weak moments? In any case, a good clarification! </div> </div> </div>Yes to leave it up to individuals except the external vs. internal thing. The Beast is an internal set of predatory instincts that vampires name to protect their Humanity, even though in reality it's just vampire instinct.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I am curious as to what you mean by resource management? </div> </div> </div>I mean that a mechanic like Limit Break turns into resource management if you try to hang long term horror on it. The tension of, &quot;it's building and going to blow!&quot; gets dull over time, and players will learn how to manage their resources to exert control over it to either avoid it or turn it towards that advantage. Like Exalted has always had Limit be something that players can avoid extremely easily. 3e's big improvements are making it more fun when it goes off (instead of a set Virtue Flaw, what your character does is situational and not always immediate), and offering a nice reward for it (Limit Break refills your Willpower when your Virtue Flaw ends which can be a big deal since the easiest way to have it build up against you can drain a lot of Willpower). If you don't want to Limit Break.. you can basically choose to never do so (not without consequence, but players have a lot of say in where that will go because they define their Intimacies and Limit Trigger).<br /> <br /> A horror game would have to be more forceful with such a mechanic, and thus detract from the fun of choosing to engage with it, and prompting players to manage the mechanics resources.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Maybe what I was looking for was something like Insanity rules that are in Call of Cthulhu type games to show a slow loss of sanity? Or maybe I wasn't understanding how much is up to the players to bring the horror to the table? </div> </div> </div>1e Morality/equivalents and 2e Integrity/equivalents are the CofD's equivalent to CoC's Insanity rules.... VtR's Humanity in particular is all about a slow loss, just not of sanity.<br /> <br /> And players don't necessarily need to bring the horror to the table. But they have to buy into the horror for it to work. If they don't actually want horror role-play you can't force it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>evildmguy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1194964-how-is-cod-played?p=1195362#post1195362\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My group and I want medium rules, where we can go more or less as the feeling hits us.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is kinda antithetical to wanting to play a specific game like Vampire. Vampire can go a lot of ways, but it can't go all the ways. There are moods it isn't going to suit. &quot;Medium&quot; doesn't really matter here. Now, the entire CofD might cover everything your group might want to do, sure, but that's why there's different rules for the different character types. Werewolves have different problems than vampires, and the mechanics represent this, allowing for a different game experience.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> From the RPGNet thread: </div> </div> </div>Is clearly VtM from the use of VtM specific traits that have different names in VtR (notably VtR dropped Influence as a standard trait and Allies in VtR combines Allies and Influence from VtM with skilled individual NPCs coming from different places).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-14T13:58:55-07:00","id":1195461,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1194964-how-is-cod-played?p=1195418#post1195418\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <span style=\"color:#252c2f\">Why would someone play that? And how could you look at vampirism myths and say things like immortality and enchantment are wholly negative?</span></div> </div> </div> </div></span></span><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1194964-how-is-cod-played?p=1195418#post1195418\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">If someone wanted to play a game about personal horror and the struggle of retaining ones humanity while bearing a terrible curse it would seem reasonable to not have superpowers coming from that curse.</span></span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1194964-how-is-cod-played?p=1195418#post1195418\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">We've had polls, very few people who actually know about the game would choose to be a vampire. The downsides outweigh the benefits and most people balk at the idea of drinking others blood to the extent vampires do. They need at least 1 Vitae a night to wake up, so you're drinking an average human once a week. Since feeding causes, at least, 1 Lethal damage a lot of players see it as a negative thing.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252c2f\">And the covenants are support groups. They represent ways people have dealt with the chaos of life. Religion, spirituality, hierarchy, community, science. They help you deal with how to be what you are, because there's no coming back. And it's realistic. Because once you get people in a strange situation they're going to find ways to adapt to it. Even the 'sitting down and giving up' people have an option in going into torpor or becoming draugr.</span><br /> </span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">to me it's about the OPs question on why he wasn't able to convey a sense of personal horror. If your player's look at disciplines and the inherent benefits of Vita and all they have to do is drink a little blood, not even kill, doesn't seem that bad. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">Couple that with the Covenant philosophy your character might pick up, a lot of people might end up playing someone who is rather fine with being a vampire or might think exploring the inhuman side of it is more interesting then playing up the struggle for humanity. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">Also regarding polls, you have to keep in mind that most average players don't delve deep into the core material and then argue on forums about it. Also huge difference between what you want to explore in roleplaying and what you want to be in real life <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></span></span></span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1194964-how-is-cod-played?p=1195418#post1195418\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">These arguments are so weird. Yes, 'the right situation' might arise. That's what plots are. That's why there's not an abundance of stories, movies, tv shows, that focus on a completely normal 9 to 5 where nothing happens except the routine. It's why even reality tv shows have scripts and go out of their way to pick dramatic people for their casts, to make things happen. And so things happen, this isn't some 'trap' Requiem pulls on you, it's expected by sitting at a table and playing a game.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">You have to look at what criticism I was answering there. </span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">My initial argument was that a game about struggling against an inner beast and not loosing ones humanity is perfectly well served by simply playing humans in the \u201cright situation\u201d.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">Counterargument: Wh</span><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><i>at vampirism adds is the guarantee that the player characters are always in this &quot;right situation&quot; you speak of.</i></span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">I say it's not, you can rather easily create a vampire who doesn't have to struggle. Without what happens at the table. So again you might just play a human. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman, serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\">What makes playing a Vampire interesting then, to me, and I think others, probably also the OPs players, is because you want to be a Vampire in game. The amount of personal horror coming from that then is limited. You also might be more interested in exploring what it's like to be an inhuman monster.</span></span></span><br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23979-traldar","name":"Traldar","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-14T14:46:07-07:00","id":1195478,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Traldar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1194964-how-is-cod-played?p=1195461#post1195461\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If someone wanted to play a game about personal horror and the struggle of retaining ones humanity while bearing a terrible curse it would seem reasonable to not have superpowers coming from that curse.</div> </div> </div> </div>This isn't that game, as said before, vampire has highs and lows. You're immortal, but you're always hungry for blood. You're never going to really get tired, but you're surrounded by monsters. You can push aside all the hardships normal humans have to deal with, but doing so causes you to lose more and more humanity.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> to me it's about the OPs question on why he wasn't able to convey a sense of personal horror. If your player's look at disciplines and the inherent benefits of Vita and all they have to do is drink a little blood, not even kill, doesn't seem that bad. </div> </div> </div>Sure. It's not that bad. Your girlfriend catches you cheating on her. Just use Dominate and make her forget. You don't work anymore but you want that new phone, just use Obfuscation and steal it. Your girlfriend catches you cheating on her again, just blood bind her and now she's hopelessly devoted to you. Get in a car accident? No big deal, now you just use more blood to heal. Some hunters saw you and now they're after you? Just rip them in half with Vigor. And congratulations, you're an inhuman monster who enslaves and kills people.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Couple that with the Covenant philosophy your character might pick up, a lot of people might end up playing someone who is rather fine with being a vampire or might think exploring the inhuman side of it is more interesting then playing up the struggle for humanity. </div> </div> </div>Maybe they will. But exploring the inhuman side of it has its drawbacks, there's an inherent inhumanity in learning blood sorcery, this is why learning it risks lowering your humanity.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also regarding polls, you have to keep in mind that most average players don't delve deep into the core material and then argue on forums about it. Also huge difference between what you want to explore in roleplaying and what you want to be in real life <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> </div> </div> </div>I can tell. They should read the book. At least the core. I'm sorry if some people find it too hard, but reading the instructions is an important part of playing a game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> My initial argument was that a game about struggling against an inner beast and not loosing ones humanity is perfectly well served by simply playing humans in the \u201cright situation\u201d </div> </div> </div>Not really. Humanity is a wide and varied thing. Basically everything we've done in the real world, from being explorers and astronauts, to serial killers and cannibalistic warlords, are are all done by humans. It wouldn't matter what you did as a normal human in the 'right situation,' if you went to sleep still a human you're not losing your humanity.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Counterargument: What vampirism adds is the guarantee that the player characters are always in this &quot;right situation&quot; you speak of. </div> </div> </div>Vampirism forms the framework. The simple fact that you're playing a game gives you the 'right situation.'<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I say it's not, you can rather easily create a vampire who doesn't have to struggle. Without what happens at the table. So again you might just play a human. </div> </div> </div>We have rules for playing a human. If you want to explore horror through playing a human, just grab Chronicles of Darkness. It's the core of the entire system. But it's a completely different game. And you can't easily create a vampire who doesn't have to struggle, because eventually the vampire needs more. This is exemplified through mechanics, with the constant Vitae requirement simply to be active and the increasing needs of blood as one gets older. It's also in evidence through the story, where things happen to you so your life as a vampire isn't just about 'waking up at sunset, watching tv for hours, then going to sleep at sunrise.' Things happen, just like in real life.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What makes playing a Vampire interesting then, to me, and I think others, probably also the OPs players, is because you want to be a Vampire in game. The amount of personal horror coming from that then is limited. You also might be more interested in exploring what it's like to be an inhuman monster. </div> </div> </div>It's focused but I wouldn't say it's limited. You're not dealing with the same problems you would in Mage or Promethean. But vampires have a lot of options when it comes to horror. And the sheer fact that they can do and survive more than a human raises their upper limits. Humans can't just look into someone's eyes and alter their minds forever. They can't feed them a drop of blood and turn them into a lovesick addict. But vampires can, and these are monstrous things to do.<br /> <br /> Also, I'm not sure how you're posting this, but there's no reason to change fonts, colors and sizes for every line or half-line you post.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-14T15:19:00-07:00","id":1195495,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>evildmguy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1194964-how-is-cod-played?p=1195245#post1195245\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The reason I was thinking Willpower is powerful is because I thought it could be used to either write a scene or auto succeed? If I'm mixing my editions, then oops!</div> </div> </div> </div>This has never been a thing in any edition.<br /> <br /> I have to admit I skimmed but I will say that you also seem to be equating the Morality trait with its alternatives, but they aren't the same. Humanity 7 != Morality 7. Humanity 7 is just the rough level of detachment from humanity an average vampire experiences shortly after their Embrace. Being Morality 1 or Morality 10 before the embrace doesn't make you any more or less detached from your former Humanity (as a state of existence).<br /> <br /> Honestly, having this kind of discussion about 1E is just...meh. It's like a 15 year old game at this point and it's flaws are glaringly obvious in hindsight. 2E has some flaws, but does an immeasurably better job reinforcing its themes with the way its mechanics interact.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3570,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3570-lnodiv","user":"lnodiv","date":". 03-14-2018, 06:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3570-lnodiv","name":"lnodiv","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3570_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1607"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-14T17:56:09-07:00","id":1195549,"title":"","content":"I do think that you would enjoy a read of 2e. It does a MUCH better job at addressing many of your complaints. <br /> <br /> I'd also like to make one point about why they start at Humanity 7. The idea isn't simply to play as inhuman monsters who vaguely look at humans as tools, as sources of sustenance, as pawns, but not as people. The idea is to show the descent into that, to start out still seeing your old girlfriend once and a while, and slowly watching the terrible things that being a vampire makes you do change your mindset, until you suddenly realized that you just snapped a man's neck and mindwiped your girlfriend multiple times, and you know what you don't even care. <br /> <br /> The horror isn't in just BEING a monster. The horror is in BECOMING a monster, in being able to see the difference between then and now, in knowing that it was always inevitable, in seeing how naive you once were in thinking you could still hold on to your humanity. <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2291-gwyn-chan-r-gwyll","name":"Gwyn chan 'r Gwyll","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-14T18:00:23-07:00","id":1195550,"title":"","content":"I'd like to point out another obstacle for the adoption of personal horror in an RPG table environment is that it requires a heavier commitment from players into the proverbial suspension of disbelief in order to achieve a greater sense of immersion in the game.<br /> <br /> Not every player is prepared for that (some people can't stand scary movies and all their sub-genres). It's also something that comes more easily with with practice to ST and players alike. STs need to learn the kind of thing that may afflict characters on a deep emotional level, balancing on the razor's edge for avoiding actually presenting them with unacceptable situations. Thus if the player is in video-game mindset, this is unlikely to come to pass.<br /> <br /> Players need a little bit of directing by that point. It's why I love conditions. I can hand out a card to a player that reads they're freakin' spooked: they'll learn to act the role, practice some method acting, get immersed more easily.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12191,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","user":"Malus","date":". 03-14-2018, 09:02 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-15T10:26:46-07:00","id":1195781,"title":"","content":"Um . . . Wow. Some good responses and I thank all of you for them. <br /> <br /> I disagree with some points and assumptions people made but don't want to discuss that here. <br /> <br /> I'm glad to see the differences between the systems and what they tried to do versus how well it worked for others. How it worked for myself as well. <br /> <br /> My apologies for asking basic questions or implying things that people didn't infer. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5581-evildmguy","name":"evildmguy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5581_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-15T21:56:13-07:00","id":1195964,"title":"","content":"Well, now I know to avoid RPG.net for advice on CofD games...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-16T03:28:10-07:00","id":1196004,"title":"","content":"They're not all that bad. That's where Princess: the Hopeful was created","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>A god is just a monster you kneel to. </b>- ArcaneArts, Quoting &quot;Fall of Gods&quot; <div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/pub/6.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7920-master-aquatosic","name":"Master Aquatosic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7920_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2369"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-16T15:01:59-07:00","id":1196236,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Master Aquatosic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1194964-how-is-cod-played?p=1196004#post1196004\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They're not all that bad. That's where Princess: the Hopeful was created</div> </div> </div> </div>Look, I <i>like</i> Princess, but I'm still not going to count that as a mark toward their favor. Princess definitely has its' flaws. some of which are rooted in the community that made it. 